CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: mechanism
SCALE_REALISM: L0 evidence is enough for a real fjall mechanism bug and a realistic un-fsync'd Buffer-mode crash signature, but insufficient for GREEN. The packet has 50-key corruption tests, one dm-flakey dropped-writeback crash, and a labeled reorder simulation on real journal bytes. It does not yet demonstrate real post-SyncAll-fsync-completed loss under a reordered device/barrier violation, so the strongest durability-contract claim remains unproven at realistic storage-failure scale.
RATIONALE: Prompt version v001. The exact fjall source path supports the location-dependent behavior: JournalReader truncates to last_valid_pos and returns None on decode errors/EOF-like framing failures, JournalBatchReader on_close silently discards an unterminated batch, and only End checksum mismatch returns JournalRecovery(ChecksumMismatch) (https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/blob/3.1.4/src/journal/reader.rs, https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/blob/3.1.4/src/journal/batch_reader.rs). PersistMode::Buffer is documented as not power-loss durable and implemented as Ok(()) after flushing only process buffers when dirty; SyncAll calls fsync (https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/blob/3.1.4/src/journal/writer.rs). Fjall issue #53 already reports truncating/missing-terminator warnings, but as a cosmetic/user-warning issue, not the clean-open location-asymmetry claim (https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/issues/53). Issues #112/#113 cover a different lost-write/journal-truncation bug path (https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/issues/112, https://github.com/fjall-rs/fjall/pull/113). Existing systems show this is not a systems design insight by itself: RocksDB explicitly exposes WAL recovery policy and warns corruption can stop/open differently by mode, while LevelDB has long tolerated incomplete trailing records. The fjall-specific mechanism is real, but the artifact currently reads as a bug/mechanism report with an important missing L1 proof, not a fully demonstrated systems insight/design.
